1
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:45,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say you understand what the middle way is and you have clear reasoning that the only reason you suffer is because of your attitude in regard to feelings, you know, in a nutshell.

2
00:00:46,357 --> 00:00:48,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's clear to you and you've reasoned it.

3
00:00:49,513 --> 00:00:49,994
[SPEAKER_00]: In all ways.

4
00:00:51,315 --> 00:00:53,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you think, but is it?

5
00:00:54,998 --> 00:00:58,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the case that my attitude is the cause of my suffering?

6
00:00:58,442 --> 00:00:59,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it really?

7
00:00:59,463 --> 00:01:00,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that really true?

8
00:01:01,545 --> 00:01:06,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the middle way the way to free myself from that suffering?

9
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:08,071
[SPEAKER_00]: To end craving?

10
00:01:08,431 --> 00:01:09,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it really the way?

11
00:01:10,053 --> 00:01:11,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have a doubt.

12
00:01:13,576 --> 00:01:14,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, what do you do?

13
00:01:15,283 --> 00:01:21,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't... there's no confirmation that, yeah, that is the middle... this is the middle way.

14
00:01:22,167 --> 00:01:24,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what you do.

15
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:27,391
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're still doubting it, means you don't have the middle way.

16
00:01:27,991 --> 00:01:37,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have, as the suttas would say, you might have had like an intellectual grasp of it, correct grasp upon reasoning, correct reasoning.

17
00:01:38,778 --> 00:01:41,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if the doubt still

18
00:01:44,437 --> 00:01:46,397
[SPEAKER_01]: It perverts the picture around, so to speak.

19
00:01:47,097 --> 00:01:51,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if in face of doubt, you know that is the truth, means you're beyond doubt.

20
00:01:51,898 --> 00:01:53,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you might try to doubt it.

21
00:01:54,339 --> 00:01:58,700
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that you're beyond doubt, because you refuse to allow doubt to arise.

22
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,540
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the doubt, the question came up, is it?

23
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:02,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And you look at it, and it's like, yes, it is.

24
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,381
[SPEAKER_01]: And each time doubter, is it?

25
00:02:04,921 --> 00:02:05,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it is.

26
00:02:05,921 --> 00:02:07,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've seen it for yourself.

27
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:09,762
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have a doubt arising, it says, is it?

28
00:02:10,122 --> 00:02:11,142
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, well, I don't know.

29
00:02:11,382 --> 00:02:12,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the work

30
00:02:13,367 --> 00:02:15,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't stop doing it because you doubt it.

31
00:02:15,688 --> 00:02:17,309
[SPEAKER_01]: You do it more because you doubt it.

32
00:02:20,070 --> 00:02:31,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yes, that intellectual reasoning, that intellectual grasp of... it makes sense that the suffering is rooted in my attitude, not in the feelings or in the world.

33
00:02:31,654 --> 00:02:34,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that is a kind of a necessary prerequisite.

34
00:02:35,476 --> 00:02:41,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Having that intellectual grasp of what the right view is, gives you the direction to develop the right view and hence go beyond doubt.

35
00:02:44,737 --> 00:02:46,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to go beyond doubt?

36
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:55,095
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how to go beyond doubt is to get that intellectual correct reasoning first, and then, when you doubt it,

37
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:57,051
[SPEAKER_01]: You allow that doubt to arise.

38
00:02:57,111 --> 00:02:59,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, why do you want to go beyond doubt?

39
00:02:59,553 --> 00:03:00,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unpleasant.

40
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:03,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so the pain of the doubt is not rooted in doubt.

41
00:03:04,758 --> 00:03:11,184
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in your craving against it, which aligns itself with your intellectual understanding of what the right view is.

42
00:03:11,264 --> 00:03:12,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the root of suffering.

43
00:03:13,046 --> 00:03:15,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Not phenomena are the root of suffering.

44
00:03:15,248 --> 00:03:17,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's doubt or something more physical, doesn't matter.

45
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:19,311
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the root of suffering.

46
00:03:19,971 --> 00:03:24,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through the pain of doubt, you get to see the Four Noble Truths, for example.

47
00:03:24,635 --> 00:03:25,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Or through any pain, for that matter.

48
00:03:26,516 --> 00:03:27,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Through any discomfort.

49
00:03:27,877 --> 00:03:29,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Discomfort of sense restraint.

50
00:03:29,838 --> 00:03:36,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Anxiety that arises on account of not using pleasures as the only means of escape from the unpleasant there is in feelings, and so on.

51
00:03:39,392 --> 00:03:45,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any form of displeasure, if you suffer on account of it, you use that to see that the suffering is actually in the attitude.

52
00:03:45,695 --> 00:03:51,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You use basically that to keep applying your correctly reasoned intellectual understanding of what the right view is.

53
00:03:54,478 --> 00:03:58,420
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like in the suttas, it's found a different bit, like a dhamma nusāre, saṃsāre.

54
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,422
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not sought upon as yet, but they have understood the right view.

55
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:04,819
[SPEAKER_01]: But now they need to practice that.

56
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:17,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Either arrive at that right view through dhammanussari, like through that intellectual reasoning of the correct kind, or through faith, like, oh, they're absolutely certain that what the Buddha said must be true, and now they're applying it.

57
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:20,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So they still, both still have to do the work.

58
00:04:21,299 --> 00:04:23,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And that work is what takes you beyond doubt.

59
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:34,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Not takes you beyond ability to ask self-questions or to put your own self into a question, but it takes you beyond ability to doubt the right answer.

60
00:04:35,787 --> 00:04:37,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you see the right answer, you know it's the right answer.

61
00:04:38,108 --> 00:04:45,713
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can doubt it, and you'll happily doubt it, and the doubt can never pervert that order and put itself first, because the right answer now is established and seen through and through.

62
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:50,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you go beyond doubt through, like, repetition of understanding.

63
00:04:51,175 --> 00:04:52,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Sufficient repetition of understanding.

64
00:04:53,112 --> 00:04:54,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

65
00:04:54,553 --> 00:04:56,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma nusāri, saddha nusāri.

66
00:04:57,014 --> 00:05:00,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, intellectually, you have the idea correct.

67
00:05:00,116 --> 00:05:00,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

68
00:05:00,597 --> 00:05:02,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you also need to apply that.

69
00:05:02,077 --> 00:05:02,618
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to do it.

70
00:05:02,638 --> 00:05:04,859
[SPEAKER_00]: With no result.

71
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:07,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, exactly.

72
00:05:07,321 --> 00:05:09,122
[SPEAKER_01]: With results will come in.

73
00:05:09,142 --> 00:05:10,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's always that element of faith.

74
00:05:11,423 --> 00:05:12,904
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, we said that before.

75
00:05:13,204 --> 00:05:19,428
[SPEAKER_01]: You come and see the doctor and based on your own reasoning and knowledge that you acquired about, you know, your condition,

76
00:05:21,006 --> 00:05:25,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a pretty safe bet that out of all the doctors, this will probably be the right one.

77
00:05:25,677 --> 00:05:26,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Makes more sense.

78
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:36,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just by reputation, but even by what you can understand that his medicine is, you see, well, this is, based on everything that I know so far, this is most likely going to work.

79
00:05:37,477 --> 00:05:40,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at least, least likely to be wrong, out of everything.

80
00:05:41,079 --> 00:05:42,520
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still need to apply the medicine.

81
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:47,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you say, yes, he's right, but you're not applying medicine, it makes no difference to your condition.

82
00:05:48,202 --> 00:05:54,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Because fundamentally, the whole reason you went to the doctor is to cure yourself from your condition of dukkha.

83
00:05:55,065 --> 00:05:56,886
[SPEAKER_01]: So, either way, you have to apply it.

84
00:05:58,028 --> 00:05:59,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this doctor is the greatest.

85
00:05:59,288 --> 00:05:59,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you think so?

86
00:06:00,008 --> 00:06:00,709
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no clue.

87
00:06:01,729 --> 00:06:04,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, did you apply his teaching and confirm that he's great?

88
00:06:04,049 --> 00:06:04,509
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I haven't.

89
00:06:04,609 --> 00:06:06,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then your faith is useless and meaningless.

90
00:06:07,850 --> 00:06:24,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your faith is like, oh, I have faith that this is least likely to be wrong, and I know that based on the basis of my experience of other doctors and other applications and other medicines, okay, so let me apply this, and then I'll verify if it's right or it's wrong, if I'm cured of dukkha or I'm not cured of dukkha.

91
00:06:24,353 --> 00:06:25,513
[SPEAKER_00]: If you apply it once,

92
00:06:26,473 --> 00:06:26,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

93
00:06:27,013 --> 00:06:30,194
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole medicine tells you, you need to apply this for a sufficient amount of time.

94
00:06:30,254 --> 00:06:34,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, you don't apply it indefinitely for the rest of my life, without any confirmation.

95
00:06:34,835 --> 00:06:38,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha himself said, if you do it rightly, the results will have to come.

96
00:06:39,716 --> 00:06:43,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's also a good criteria that you can use.

97
00:06:44,838 --> 00:06:53,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, for example, if you are convinced that this is the right doctor, yet, here you are, 20 years later, you're still subject to dukkha.

98
00:06:53,829 --> 00:06:55,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is it the right doctor, though?

99
00:06:56,210 --> 00:06:57,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Have I been doing everything rightly?

100
00:06:59,810 --> 00:07:04,752
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, well, then the instruction was not right, maybe, because I'm not free from this condition.

101
00:07:05,532 --> 00:07:11,073
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes people refuse to look that, and admire the doctor for the rest of their life, and they're still not cured.

102
00:07:11,593 --> 00:07:13,994
[SPEAKER_01]: There are only two reasons you won't be cured.

103
00:07:14,014 --> 00:07:17,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Either you're doing it wrongly, the right instruction, or the instruction is not right.

104
00:07:18,875 --> 00:07:22,257
[SPEAKER_01]: And you must basically investigate both if there are no results.

105
00:07:22,538 --> 00:07:26,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, don't expect the results within one day or five days or something.

106
00:07:27,021 --> 00:07:28,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't even expect it within a year.

107
00:07:29,723 --> 00:07:33,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most people's minds today are lazy and driven to centrality.

108
00:07:33,966 --> 00:07:35,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's going to take you a bit longer than that.

109
00:07:36,107 --> 00:07:43,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, five years of doing something is like, well, am I free from doubt, free from suffering or not?

110
00:07:44,193 --> 00:07:47,275
[SPEAKER_01]: How long will I keep this faith without any verification going?

111
00:07:48,507 --> 00:07:51,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I need to re-evaluate certain things, including the doctor.

112
00:07:52,688 --> 00:08:06,731
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, the problem with that is, it gets harder to re-evaluate the doctor, if you spend more time invested in his instruction, because it feels like the conclusion of my re-evaluation might be, I have to start again.

113
00:08:06,791 --> 00:08:07,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to start again.

114
00:08:07,691 --> 00:08:09,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there you go.

115
00:08:09,432 --> 00:08:10,752
[SPEAKER_01]: You just shot yourself in the foot.

116
00:08:11,792 --> 00:08:14,853
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you always have to be willing to start again, 30 years later.

117
00:08:16,067 --> 00:08:17,168
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are not free from suffering.

118
00:08:17,188 --> 00:08:19,648
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're free from suffering, you know there is no starting again.

119
00:08:19,708 --> 00:08:20,169
[SPEAKER_01]: This is it.

120
00:08:20,209 --> 00:08:22,249
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the direction that just needs to develop further.

121
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:29,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you still doubt that you're free from suffering and are afraid to start again, practically what you are afraid of is to practice.

122
00:08:30,392 --> 00:08:32,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Because starting again is part of the practice.

123
00:08:33,373 --> 00:08:34,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Part of the finding the right doctor.

124
00:08:35,811 --> 00:08:45,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yes, if you find the right doctor, free yourself from suffering, then you might see, in hindsight, that all these previous doctors, they were not so right, actually did contribute towards your right search.

125
00:08:45,633 --> 00:08:49,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was part of the same progress, but not if you decide you're not going to start again.

126
00:08:51,394 --> 00:08:51,794
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

127
00:08:51,954 --> 00:08:55,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You remain stuck with the wrong doctor out of fear to do the work again.

128
00:08:56,495 --> 00:08:58,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You do want to get somewhere.

129
00:08:58,215 --> 00:09:01,596
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be cured of the condition that's liable to sickness, aging and death.

130
00:09:01,656 --> 00:09:03,536
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the suttas cannot be more clear on that.

131
00:09:04,793 --> 00:09:14,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here we are practicing the Buddha's teaching, seemingly, without... you know, all this is just another becoming, becoming a sort of... and yes, it's the right becoming.

132
00:09:14,738 --> 00:09:17,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You use the right becoming to overcome the wrong becoming.

133
00:09:17,719 --> 00:09:19,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, arahant goes beyond becoming.

134
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:28,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start dismissing becoming anything, because you didn't, you couldn't do it, well, basically, you're now dismissing the possibility of freedom from suffering.

135
00:09:28,764 --> 00:09:31,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, why wouldn't you want to become free from suffering?

136
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:34,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, becoming is bad.

137
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:35,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

138
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:36,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Relatively speaking.

139
00:09:38,041 --> 00:09:43,982
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't just dismiss becoming of any kind while you're still subjected to these unwholesome things.

140
00:09:44,243 --> 00:09:45,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't know the way out of suffering.

141
00:09:46,323 --> 00:09:48,444
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... it sounds like... that's...

142
00:09:48,584 --> 00:09:49,244
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an excuse.

143
00:09:49,364 --> 00:09:51,344
[SPEAKER_01]: It just basically becomes like... that's what I mean.

144
00:09:51,384 --> 00:09:52,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You got stuck with your doctor.

145
00:09:53,045 --> 00:09:57,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you feel too old or too much invested time in this direction to re-evaluate, to start again.

146
00:09:57,646 --> 00:09:58,446
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... there's no way.

147
00:09:59,106 --> 00:10:03,330
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the longer you wait to start questioning yourself rightly, the harder it will be.

148
00:10:03,350 --> 00:10:08,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the suttas say, you are accumulating burden, you know, pañca, proliferation.

149
00:10:08,775 --> 00:10:09,756
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an accumulation.

150
00:10:09,896 --> 00:10:11,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a static thing.

151
00:10:11,938 --> 00:10:13,740
[SPEAKER_01]: At any given time, you just give it all up.

152
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:13,900
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

153
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:18,945
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you've been engaging with the attitude of not giving it up, the harder it will be to give it up later on.

154
00:10:19,305 --> 00:10:22,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't wait until that burden becomes too much to give it up.

155
00:10:28,769 --> 00:10:30,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're doing things rightly...

156
00:10:30,890 --> 00:10:31,951
[SPEAKER_01]: There has to be a result.

157
00:10:32,091 --> 00:10:37,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, there's the simile in the suttas we refer to often, you know, the hand sitting on her eggs.

158
00:10:37,954 --> 00:10:44,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if she doesn't think, may these chicks pierce out safely through this shell, they will, because she's doing the work.

159
00:10:45,879 --> 00:10:54,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are doing the work, you're kind of confident about the middle way.

160
00:10:56,545 --> 00:10:57,125
[SPEAKER_01]: You're beyond doubt.

161
00:10:58,047 --> 00:10:59,668
[SPEAKER_00]: But doubt is still arising.

162
00:10:59,688 --> 00:10:59,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

163
00:11:00,048 --> 00:11:00,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear is still arising.

164
00:11:01,129 --> 00:11:01,589
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust.

165
00:11:01,850 --> 00:11:02,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Anger is still arising.

166
00:11:05,352 --> 00:11:05,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

167
00:11:05,592 --> 00:11:07,794
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you think, well, that shouldn't be the case.

168
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:09,094
[SPEAKER_00]: I should have...

169
00:11:09,114 --> 00:11:10,616
[SPEAKER_00]: I should know the truth.

170
00:11:12,337 --> 00:11:13,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you're not confident in the middle way.

171
00:11:13,998 --> 00:11:14,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

172
00:11:15,919 --> 00:11:21,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You start by saying you're confident in the middle way, but then these things arise, and you doubt, and you think you shouldn't, means you're not confident in the middle way.

173
00:11:22,223 --> 00:11:27,407
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're confident in the middle way, you would know that these things would still arise, but you know the escape from them.

174
00:11:28,605 --> 00:11:29,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You know what to do.

175
00:11:31,712 --> 00:11:32,995
[SPEAKER_00]: You know where the problem is.

176
00:11:33,075 --> 00:11:34,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, exactly.

177
00:11:34,539 --> 00:11:37,445
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem with all of those things you listed is that it makes you feel...

178
00:11:38,773 --> 00:11:39,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncomfortable.

179
00:11:39,493 --> 00:11:40,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes you suffer.

180
00:11:41,114 --> 00:11:43,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It causes a painful feeling.

181
00:11:43,634 --> 00:11:47,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know the escape in regard to pain and feeling, other than sensuality, other than giving in.

182
00:11:48,175 --> 00:11:57,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, somebody who is coming in the middle way, might become careless, lose the sense of urgency, content with knowing the escape.

183
00:11:58,337 --> 00:12:00,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, now I have confirmed that this is the right doctor.

184
00:12:00,978 --> 00:12:02,438
[SPEAKER_01]: I already felt the improvement.

185
00:12:03,563 --> 00:12:04,443
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, I get careless.

186
00:12:04,483 --> 00:12:10,006
[SPEAKER_01]: I go out, expose the wound to the sun or the dirt, as the Buddha would say, and you're not cured.

187
00:12:10,186 --> 00:12:13,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Although you know the way out, you still keep yourself sick through negligence.

188
00:12:13,787 --> 00:12:15,608
[SPEAKER_01]: But sooner or later, you have to address it.

189
00:12:15,728 --> 00:12:19,630
[SPEAKER_01]: If you... if a person truly has the right view, they cannot pretend.

190
00:12:20,390 --> 00:12:24,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And ignore the problem for, well, no more than seven lifetimes, as they say.

191
00:12:25,833 --> 00:12:26,454
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing.

192
00:12:26,474 --> 00:12:33,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you would, like, for Sotāpanna, for example, who is obviously not free from sensuality, he can still have pressing desires.

193
00:12:33,978 --> 00:12:36,399
[SPEAKER_01]: He might even give in to them out of carelessness.

194
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:40,382
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, the pain of it is understood.

195
00:12:40,522 --> 00:12:44,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the root of the pain is understood, which is your own craving.

196
00:12:46,199 --> 00:12:50,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the sensuality, not the beautiful objects, not the ugly objects, not the design or the lack of design.

197
00:12:50,944 --> 00:12:51,825
[SPEAKER_01]: The pain is understood.

198
00:12:51,845 --> 00:12:53,266
[SPEAKER_01]: The escape from the pain is understood.

199
00:12:53,566 --> 00:12:55,428
[SPEAKER_01]: The origin and the way leading out of it and so on.

200
00:12:55,528 --> 00:12:56,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all understood.

201
00:12:58,852 --> 00:13:00,794
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you know exactly where the problem is.

202
00:13:00,894 --> 00:13:03,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And by knowing where the problem is, you know how to solve the problem.

203
00:13:03,417 --> 00:13:07,421
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do also know that it will take time and the application and not becoming negligent.

204
00:13:08,181 --> 00:13:24,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because if you've been spending your whole life cultivating lust, you know, or anger, and then you've... you know the middle way, and, okay, you've confirmed it, it's not like the lust or the anger that you've been... Of course, of course.

205
00:13:24,972 --> 00:13:28,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Just knowing the escape... ...habitually doing... Yeah, it will not be... it's already significantly diminished.

206
00:13:29,175 --> 00:13:29,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

207
00:13:29,973 --> 00:13:37,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, a noble disciple who is not fully enlightened yet, can certainly still experience sensual desire and anger, but to a degree.

208
00:13:38,457 --> 00:13:44,960
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like Puthujjana's anger, and Puthujjana's sensuality, and Puthujjana's negligence, because it's like he doesn't know the way out.

209
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:47,061
[SPEAKER_01]: It's far worse.

210
00:13:47,121 --> 00:13:49,343
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only go so far.

211
00:13:49,483 --> 00:13:50,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

212
00:13:50,143 --> 00:13:55,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing the way out, it's already like a massive step towards the escape.

213
00:13:55,707 --> 00:13:56,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha himself said that.

214
00:13:57,368 --> 00:14:04,213
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, like, gaining the right view, the amount of suffering that's left for somebody with the right view, it's like seven grains of sand.

215
00:14:04,933 --> 00:14:09,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And one, without a right view, it's like the Mount Sumeru and the Suttas.

216
00:14:09,657 --> 00:14:14,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like a gigantic Himalaya... Himalaya amount of suffering, if you don't have the right view.

217
00:14:14,942 --> 00:14:18,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Just getting the right view diminishes that to seven grains of sand.

218
00:14:18,886 --> 00:14:22,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the seven grains of sand is the practice of sotāpanna to an arahant.

219
00:14:23,470 --> 00:14:24,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust arises.

220
00:14:24,935 --> 00:14:28,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust arises, ill will arises, and so on.

221
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:29,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Hindrances arise.

222
00:14:29,562 --> 00:14:30,844
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a desire for pleasure.

223
00:14:31,665 --> 00:14:33,207
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a desire to avoid pain.

224
00:14:33,227 --> 00:14:33,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

225
00:14:33,728 --> 00:14:38,715
[SPEAKER_01]: All, basically, all, like, all a noble disciple would have to do is practice sense restraint.

226
00:14:39,096 --> 00:14:39,737
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't need to.

227
00:14:40,898 --> 00:14:44,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, be dealing with the doubt as well, whether this is the way out, because he knows.

228
00:14:44,140 --> 00:14:51,263
[SPEAKER_01]: All he needs to do is not give in to that impulse towards lust, or ill-will, or negligence.

229
00:14:51,364 --> 00:14:58,727
[SPEAKER_01]: All he needs to do is abstain from the impulse, and that already results in clarity of what's there, understanding of dukkha, and so on.

230
00:14:59,568 --> 00:15:07,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nekama-sankhapa developed sufficiently in thoughts of renunciation, arahantic, for somebody with the right view.

231
00:15:11,878 --> 00:15:14,061
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the work is for a noble disciple.

232
00:15:14,301 --> 00:15:16,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I thought about enduring and so on.

233
00:15:17,805 --> 00:15:18,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Allowing things to endure.

234
00:15:18,846 --> 00:15:20,068
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the beginning of it.

235
00:15:20,388 --> 00:15:23,472
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that doubt is always a tricky one, isn't it?

236
00:15:23,552 --> 00:15:31,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... Well, yeah, you feel like I must address this, but the point realizes, well, no, the only reason I want to address this is because I want to get rid of the pain of it, not find the right answer.

237
00:15:33,289 --> 00:15:34,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you already know the right answer.

238
00:15:34,570 --> 00:15:40,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when... when that doubt is sort of... when people like... like... well, basically, irrationally, neurotically.

239
00:15:40,415 --> 00:15:41,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what if?

240
00:15:41,236 --> 00:15:41,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what if?

241
00:15:42,037 --> 00:15:42,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what if?

242
00:15:42,677 --> 00:15:51,205
[SPEAKER_01]: And each time you provide the right answer, and you know it's the right answer, if you were not affected emotionally by that doubt, if somebody were to ask you that same question, you would give the same answer.

243
00:15:51,265 --> 00:15:52,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that is the truth.

244
00:15:53,246 --> 00:15:57,390
[SPEAKER_01]: But your weakness is not lie... it doesn't lie in the fact that you don't have the right answer.

245
00:15:57,510 --> 00:15:59,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Lies in the fact that you can't bear the pain

246
00:16:01,139 --> 00:16:06,302
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why none of the answers can satisfy it, because you're not really answering for the sake of answer.

247
00:16:06,582 --> 00:16:10,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You're answering it for the sake of not feeling the pain, and that doesn't work.

248
00:16:10,684 --> 00:16:12,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You will feel the pain, because you act out of pain.

249
00:16:13,086 --> 00:16:21,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your answer is you're acting out of pain, because I don't want the pain, which means, oh, I need to endure the pain, not revolve around answering the doubt.

250
00:16:22,431 --> 00:16:24,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you endure the pain sufficiently,

251
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:31,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice basically thoughts of renunciation of your answering the doubt, refraining from it.

252
00:16:31,905 --> 00:16:34,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, oh, this is the escape of doubt.

253
00:16:34,527 --> 00:16:35,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the escape of dukkha.

254
00:16:35,948 --> 00:16:36,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the escape of pain.

255
00:16:37,189 --> 00:16:38,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Not it's finding the right answer.

256
00:16:41,392 --> 00:16:43,194
[SPEAKER_01]: That's rooted in not wanting the pain.

257
00:16:45,695 --> 00:16:46,716
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you don't want the pain?

258
00:16:48,277 --> 00:16:50,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I don't know the way out of it.

259
00:16:50,039 --> 00:16:54,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Or because I do know the way out of it, but I'm just impatient and I want it to be... go away.

260
00:16:54,362 --> 00:16:55,183
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to have to deal with it.

261
00:16:56,028 --> 00:16:57,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so you're not practicing rightly then.

262
00:16:57,929 --> 00:16:59,890
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the pleasure of not having doubt.

263
00:17:00,410 --> 00:17:01,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I want the pleasure of not having pain.

264
00:17:03,892 --> 00:17:06,874
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason doubt is a problem is because you don't want to deal with the pain of it.

265
00:17:07,214 --> 00:17:07,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

266
00:17:09,455 --> 00:17:14,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, by the way, even in mundane terms, not just in terms of dhamma and right view and sotāpati.

267
00:17:14,728 --> 00:17:25,693
[SPEAKER_01]: When people... when people's minds revolve around doubt, they revolve around it because of the pain of it, not because of the not enough information or clarity in regard to the answer in the doubt.

268
00:17:26,554 --> 00:17:36,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why doubt is a hindrance, because of that emotional... Let's see, you act for it because as if... it presents itself as if it needs an answer for clarity, but in reality, it's masked.

269
00:17:36,798 --> 00:17:43,742
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is the pain, and each time you try to give an answer, all you do is act out of not wanting pain, and that's why pain will be there.

270
00:17:44,489 --> 00:17:45,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're feeding it.

271
00:17:46,931 --> 00:17:47,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So, endure the doubt.

272
00:17:51,514 --> 00:17:52,675
[SPEAKER_01]: What if my chicks don't pierce?

273
00:17:53,176 --> 00:17:53,876
[SPEAKER_01]: What if they do this?

274
00:17:54,136 --> 00:17:54,717
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I do that?

275
00:17:54,897 --> 00:17:55,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stand up.

276
00:17:55,838 --> 00:17:59,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the chick, the hen, gets up, sits, goes, moves the eggs.

277
00:18:00,101 --> 00:18:05,145
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the chicks will not pierce safely from the eggs.

278
00:18:05,805 --> 00:18:09,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Because out of her doubt, she keeps moving around and not sitting on the eggs.

279
00:18:10,733 --> 00:18:12,254
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't matter if she doubts.

280
00:18:12,894 --> 00:18:13,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Will my chicks pierce?

281
00:18:14,034 --> 00:18:14,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Will they not?

282
00:18:14,975 --> 00:18:15,895
[SPEAKER_01]: What will happen to them?

283
00:18:16,015 --> 00:18:22,418
[SPEAKER_01]: If she doesn't move on account of doubt, and induce it, and keep sitting on the chicks, they will pierce out.

284
00:18:22,718 --> 00:18:27,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And when she sees that once, twice, three times, doubt will become meaningless.

285
00:18:27,421 --> 00:18:28,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And you went beyond doubt.

286
00:18:29,722 --> 00:18:32,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, what if, what if, there's no what if.

287
00:18:32,223 --> 00:18:37,045
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done this sufficiently enough that I know what I need to do for chicks to pierce out safely.

288
00:18:39,288 --> 00:18:48,072
[SPEAKER_01]: But if she keeps fidgeting around and moving, she's actually not doing the work on account of trying to fix the doubt and deal with it and make sure that her chicks pierce out safely.

289
00:18:48,332 --> 00:18:48,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Ironically enough.

290
00:18:49,753 --> 00:18:52,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I must find the answer in order to practice rightly.

291
00:18:52,334 --> 00:18:54,335
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I'm not practicing rightly.

292
00:18:54,455 --> 00:18:58,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I keep acting out of pain of doubt.

293
00:19:03,339 --> 00:19:05,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why acting out of doubt is always unwholesome.

294
00:19:06,441 --> 00:19:08,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And even in our vinaya rules,

295
00:19:09,322 --> 00:19:11,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out of doubt is wrongdoing.

296
00:19:11,664 --> 00:19:19,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if what you did turned out not to be against the rules, the fact that you acted out of doubt was a minor breach of the rules.

297
00:19:20,909 --> 00:19:22,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you doubt, you don't do it.

298
00:19:23,711 --> 00:19:26,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it might be, oh, maybe, maybe it would be the right answer.

299
00:19:26,673 --> 00:19:30,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's rooted in the doubt, which means my priority is not the right answer.

300
00:19:30,715 --> 00:19:31,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the clarity of understanding.

301
00:19:31,876 --> 00:19:34,217
[SPEAKER_01]: My priority is getting rid of the discomfort of the doubt.

302
00:19:34,698 --> 00:19:35,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's wrong.

